<node id="1185750" title="Re: Joining multiple lines together while parsing" created="2017-03-24 06:24:06" updated="2017-03-24 06:24:06" _doctext_mode="lenient"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="5348">Corion</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;p&gt;I think your regular expression here:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
my %row = m/\s*(\w+)\S*\s+(\S.*)/g;
&lt;/c&gt;
&lt;p&gt;... doesn't extract everything because it's in "dot-does-not-match-newline" mode.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;c&gt;/s&lt;/c&gt; switch makes "." match newlines ("treat variable as 's'ingle line") and thus could fix your problem:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
$_ = &lt;&lt;DATA;
    Detail:              Some really nice infos these are 
    Info:              This is a problem
                       but there is a solution
DATA
my %row = m/\s*(\w+):\s+(\S[^:]+)/gs;
&lt;/c&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But then, that means that &lt;c&gt;Info&lt;/c&gt; gets gobbled up again into the description part.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Personally, I would do manual line-by-line parsing instead of using one regular expression to capture everything:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
$_ = &lt;&lt;DATA;
    Detail:              Some really nice infos these are 
    Info:              This is a problem
                       but there is a solution
DATA
my %row;

my $curr;
for (split /\n/) {
    if( /^\s*(\w+):\s+(.*)/ ) {
        $curr = $1;
        $row{ $curr } = $2;
    } elsif( $curr and /^\s*(.*)/ ) {
        $row{ $curr } .= ' ' . $1;
    } else {
        die "Unknown input data [$_]";
    };
};
&lt;/c&gt;</doctext>
